---
title: Litestone — the schema that enforces itself — FrontierJS
description: Litestone is a schema-first SQLite layer for Bun. One .lite file declares your data, who may touch it and what happens to it over its life, and the types, validators, migrations and access rules all follow.
render: static
---
<!--
  The Litestone page: the Data realm, argued in five sections. Prerendered,
  no script beyond the layout's theme swatches. Same shape as
  routes/basecamp.mesa; the shared structure is components/ProductFrame.mesa.

  ── Section contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Each section is a component in components/litestone/, and its root is
  `<section id=… class="bc-section">` holding a `.container.stack`:

    p.eyebrow                       the topic's name
    h2                              the claim, in the reader's words
    p.lede                          one or two sentences, one idea
    .bc-split
      ul.bc-points > li             <strong>head.</strong> ONE or TWO sentences,
                                    3–4 of them; a point over four lines is cut
      figure.bc-proof               ONE proof: real schema lines, real output of
                                    a run, or a .flow of real steps, with a
                                    figcaption saying where it came from
    p.bc-more                       "Read more:" links into the repo docs

  Every claim and every code line is lifted from the package docs or code, or
  from a run, and is something that works TODAY. Litestone is shipped, so the
  page can say so, but a claim still needs a doc fence, a test or a run behind
  it. Direction is labeled as direction. No comparison with another ORM unless
  the cell was re-verified.
-->
